> chaotic wizard? I like being able to kill shopkeepers

I always just let my pets do that.  If you
consistently pick up all the tripe rations
you find and throw each of them to the
next domestic carnivore you see (one tripe
ration per animal), a trip through the Mines
(with all those polymorph traps and the
occasional chameleon corpse) will usually
net you at least one pet strong enough to
kill shopkeepers with impunity.  I've had
pet dragons as a result of polymorph on
numerous occasions, a barbed devil once,
fire elementals  several times, a pyrolisk
(whom I named Bernie), a chameleon who
lasted a surprisingly long time, a centaur,
and I don't remember what all.  Of course,
sometimes a pet turns into something so
weak as to be useless (kobold, woodchuck,
fungus, etc) but in that case you just leave it
behind and throw tripe at the next dog you see.

Also, if you happen to get yourself polymorphed
before you find magic resistance, and you happen
to turn into anything powerful that can lay eggs,
and your character is female, carry several of the
eggs in your pack until they hatch.  Carrying eggs
is another way I've gotten pet dragons on more
than one occasion.  Once I even got a pet dragon
from an egg that I found on the dungeon floor and
carried around.

Later in the game you may be able to directly
tame powerful monsters with reasonably low
magic resistance (e.g., minotaurs, purple
worms, elementals, etc), but by then you've
usually killed most of the shopkeepers you
want to kill.  OTOH, if you have more than
one aligned priest, it can be handy to have
a pet kill one off.  It's possible to get as far
as the Valley before you find your second
aligned priest, and by that time you may be
able to cast charm monster.

Having a powerful pet does mean you have to
obsessively lock the doors to shops and temples
whose attendants you *don't* want dead, but it
also turns a magic whistle into a powerful
weapon. IMO, it's worth it on the whole.


